<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peace of Cambrai</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1529">1529</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Ladies' Peace, Treaty of Cambrai (1529) · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-league-of-cognac">War of the League of Cognac</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Peace of Cambrai, called the Ladies' Peace, was signed on 3 August 1529 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i">Francis I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, negotiated by Louise of Savoy and Margaret of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. Francis gave up his claims in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and his suzerainty over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> and Artois and paid a ransom for his sons held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, while Charles dropped his claim to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peace-of-cambrai.jpg" alt="Peace of Cambrai" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernard van Orley · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bernaerd_van_Orley_002.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
